med","webcast":false,"url":"https://broxbourne.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-03","committee":"Cabinet","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://broxbourne.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-10","committee":"Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://broxbourne.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-12-01","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://broxbourne.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-06-23","committee":"Audit and Standards Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://broxbourne.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-14","committee":"Cabinet","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://broxbourne.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-21","committee":"Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://broxbourne.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-28","committee":"Council","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://broxbourne.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-28","committee":"Council","time":"7.20 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://broxbourne.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Broxbourne Council to repay \u00a351.65m borrowing within 12 months","lead":"Broxbourne Council's entire \u00a351.65m borrowing portfolio matures between April and September 2026, meaning the loans must be repaid and replaced\u2014a concentration of debt repayment the council's treasury report describes as deliberate strategy to retain borrowing flexibility. The council's investment portfolio\u2014money held in short-term investments to create an emergency financial cushion\u2014has declined by \u00a38m to \u00a354m during the year. The available financial buffer against the borrowing has thus shrunk to \u00a32.35m. The council generated \u00a32.4m of investment income at a 4.25% average return during the year, according to the treasury management report, demonstrating active cash management despite the portfolio decline.","iso":"2026-09-22","paper_title":"TREASURYMANAGEMENTREPORT","paper_url":"https://broxbourne.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s10556/10556.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"TREASURYMANAGEMENTREPORT","paper_url":"https://broxbourne.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s10556/10556.pdf","headline":"The council generated \u00a32.4m of investment income at 4.25% average return despite the portfolio declining","detail":"From an average investment portfolio of \u00a356.1m, the council achieved 4.25% average return, generating \u00a32,386,414 direct income plus \u00a32,808,721 total including cash balances. This demonstrates active cash management while investments contracted.","category":"income","matters":"Investment income supports the capital programme and helps offset borrowing costs. The return achieved shows effective money market engagement.","figures":"\u00a356.1 million average portfolio; 4.25% average return; \u00a32,386,414 investment income; \u00a32,808,721 total","quote":"The average rate of return on the Council's cash investments in the 2025/26 financial year was 4.25%, generating investment income of \u00a32,386,414. Total investment income including interest on cash in-hand balances was \u00a32,808,721.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"TREASURYMANAGEMENTREPORT","paper_url":"https://broxbourne.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s10556/10556.pdf","headline":"The council's investment portfolio declined by \u00a38 million (12.9%) during the year","detail":"Total investments fell from \u00a362m at 1 April 2025 to \u00a354m at 31 March 2026. The council attributes this to cash flow movements without elaboration. This reduces the liquidity buffer against the \u00a351.65m borrowing position from \u00a310.35m to \u00a32.35m.","category":"reserves","matters":"Lenders monitor liquid reserves as an indicator of financial resilience. A significant reduction in invested cash could signal capital spending, working capital changes, or cash pressure\u2014relevant to assessing refinancing capacity.","figures":"\u00a362 million opening; \u00a354 million closing; \u00a38 million reduction","quote":"Total investments stood at \u00a354million at 31 March 2026 down from \u00a362 million at the start of the financial year due to cash flow movements.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"TREASURYMANAGEMENTREPORT","paper_url":"https://broxbourne.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s10556/10556.pdf","headline":"The council's entire \u00a351.65m borrowing portfolio is due to mature and require refinancing within 12 months","detail":"The maturity structure table shows 100% of borrowing concentrated in the under-12-months bucket, with 0% in any longer period. Appendix D confirms all loans mature between April and September 2026. The council describes this as deliberate strategy to retain borrowing flexibility.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Refinancing risk is a key credit metric. A council with all debt due within 12 months must refinance \u00a351.65m within the coming year, creating material liquidity demand and exposure to interest rate movements.","figures":"\u00a351.65 million total; 100% under 12 months; 0% all other maturity periods","quote":"Maturity Structure of Borrowing: This indicator is required as a measure of exposure to refinancing risk.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-21","committee":"Scrutiny Committee","paper_title":"2025-26 Q4 Revenue Monitor","paper_url":"https://broxbourne.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s10337/10337.pdf","headline":"General Fund services overspent by \u00a3799k, only achieving balance through one-off grants and interest income","detail":"The Cost of Services (General Fund) overran budget by \u00a3799,306. After accounting for Sport and Leisure trading profits, the council faced a \u00a3670k shortfall. This was covered only through additional interest income (\u00a3578,787) and a National Insurance grant (\u00a3141,785), leaving a narrow \u00a350k surplus.","category":"overspend","matters":"The council's operational services ran approximately 9% over budget. The final surplus depends on interest income and discretionary grants, not on operational control. This suggests structural cost pressures or budget management challenges.","figures":"Cost of Services overspend \u00a3799,306; additional interest income \u00a3578,787; National Insurance grant \u00a3141,785; final surplus \u00a350,460","quote":"This is offset by grant and additional interest income giving a final surplus position of \u00a350k.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-21","committee":"Scrutiny Committee","paper_title":"2025-26 Q4 Revenue Monitor","paper_url":"https://broxbourne.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s10337/10337.pdf","headline":"\u00a3468k overspend in Property Services due to business rates on empty commercial properties and lost rental income","detail":"The Resources directorate overspent by \u00a3387k overall, with Property Services showing an overspend of \u00a3468,247. This is driven by costs of maintaining empty commercial properties and the resulting loss of rental income that would normally offset those costs.","category":"income","matters":"Empty commercial property holdings represent dead weight on council finances and suggest either portfolio management issues or a struggling commercial real estate market in the borough. Lost rental income reduces the council's revenue base.","figures":"\u00a3468,247 overspend in Property Services","quote":"This is mainly due to the cost of business rates for empty commercial properties and the associated loss of rental income.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-22"},"name":"Broxbourne","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-22","since_iso":"2026-08-23","distress":false,"headline":"The council generated \u00a32.4m of investment income at 4.25% average return despite the portfolio declining","lead":"Investment income supports the capital programme and helps offset borrowing costs. The return achieved shows effective money market engagement.","also":["2 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Cabinet on 2026-09-22","n_findings":3,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"TREASURYMANAGEMENTREPORT","top_paper_url":"https://broxbourne.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s10556/10556.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":66,"news_drafts_total":1}}